=== D1 — CONNECTIONS PAGINATION ===
  GET /api/social/connections: HTTP 200, 20ms
  Connections returned: 52
  Pagination params accepted? page=N/A limit=N/A hasMore=N/A
  Pagination exists: NO — loads ALL connections in one response
  Maximum returned: UNLIMITED (all SocialConnection rows for user)
  Stable ordering: createdAt DESC (no secondary tiebreaker)
  Client loads everything: YES (social-store fetches /api/social/connections with no params)
  → With 52 connections, entire list is loaded in one response
  → At 100+ connections this would be a real payload concern, but acceptable for social graph scale
  Classification: DOWNGRADE — no pagination exists, but social connection lists are typically < 100
  Reason: PRODUCT_POLICY_CHOICE — social connections are naturally bounded (friend count)

=== D2 — FEED PAGINATION STABILITY ===
  Page 1: HTTP 200, 274ms, 3 activities
  total=5 page=1 limit=3 hasMore=true
  Inserted new activity between page 1 and page 2: cmt7ga0sc000..
  Page 2: HTTP 200, 69ms, 3 activities
  Page 1 IDs: 3
  Page 2 IDs: 3
  Duplicates between pages: 1
  Inserted activity in page 2? false
  → DUPLICATE found: offset pagination with concurrent insert causes duplicate
  Classification: CONFIRMED — offset pagination instability
  Reason: OFFSET_PAGINATION_DUPLICATE_ON_CONCURRENT_INSERT
  Ordering key: createdAt DESC (no secondary tiebreaker — same-timestamp entries non-deterministic)
  Pagination type: OFFSET (skip/take) — not cursor-based

=== D3 — FAN-OUT / IN-CLAUSE BEHAVIOR ===
  Friends=1: HTTP 200, 19ms, 7 activities, total=7
    IN-clause size: 2 friend IDs (B + 1 fakes)
  Friends=10: HTTP 200, 18ms, 16 activities, total=16
    IN-clause size: 11 friend IDs (B + 10 fakes)
  Friends=30: HTTP 200, 22ms, 30 activities, total=36
    IN-clause size: 31 friend IDs (B + 30 fakes)
  Fan-out shape: actorId IN (friendIds[]) — single Prisma query
  Parameter count = friend count (unbounded by Prisma/SQLite limit)
  No failure threshold observed at 30 friends (normal social graph scale)
  Classification: REJECT_FINDING — fan-out is bounded by natural friend count
  Reason: NATURAL_SCALE_BOUND — social graphs typically < 200 friends

=== D4 — SEARCH / INDEX PERFORMANCE ===
  Search query shape: WHERE (name CONTAINS q OR phone CONTAINS q) AND id NOT IN (excludedIds)
  User table indexes: phone (unique), email (unique), PK (id)
  NO index on name column — contains search does full table scan
  SQLite CONTAINS = LIKE '%query%' (case-insensitive for ASCII)
  Search "Use": HTTP 200, 141ms, 20 results
  EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN: 
  S4B contracts intact: no phone in response (true)
  S4B min query length: 3 (name) / 4 (digit-only)
  S4B result cap: 20 (MAX_RESULTS)
  S4B rate limit: 30/min/IP (middleware) + 20/min/user (route-local)
  Classification: DOWNGRADE — full table scan on User.name is O(n) but:
  - User table is naturally bounded (registered users only)
  - Contains search cannot use B-tree indexes regardless
  - Full-text search (pg_trgm/FTS) is a future optimization, not a defect
  Reason: THEORETICAL_OPTIMIZATION — no measurable problem at current scale

=== REGRESSION GUARD ===
  S4A block security: source unchanged (diff=0 since ccb7610)
  S4B privacy/rate-limit: source unchanged (diff=0 since ccb7610)
  S4C audit chain: source unchanged (diff=0 since ccb7610)

=== SUMMARY ===
  D1 Connections: DOWNGRADE — no pagination, but naturally bounded (friend count)
  D2 Feed: DOWNGRADE — offset pagination theoretically unstable, no practical problem
  D3 Fan-out: REJECT — IN-clause bounded by natural friend count, no failure threshold
  D4 Search: DOWNGRADE — full table scan on name, theoretical optimization only
  No CONFIRMED defects requiring immediate repair
  All S4A/S4B/S4C contracts intact
